The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
putname(name);
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
```
Reason:
The `putname(name);` call was executed before the function could determine whether to retry the operation. This premature cleanup of the `name` variable led to a use-after-free situation, where the variable was accessed after it had been freed, resulting in undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
putname(name);
```
Reason:
By moving the `putname(name);` call to after the retry check, the code ensures that the `name` variable is only freed after it is confirmed that no retry will occur. This prevents the use-after-free error, as the variable remains valid for the duration of the retry logic.

